# Send email through Bird

Before you start, verify a sending domain under **Email** > **Domains** in your Bird workspace. Bird only sends from verified domains and checks the DKIM, return-path, and DMARC records it gives you.

1. Go to **Messaging** > **Providers** > **Add provider** and choose **Bird** under **Email**.
2. On Appwrite Cloud, click **Connect with Bird** on the **One-click setup** card and authorize Appwrite in Bird.
3. Pick a verified **Domain** and click **Generate API key**.
4. Review the filled-in fields and click **Create provider**.

{% only_dark %}
![The Configure Bird step for the email provider after connecting Bird, with a verified domain selected and the Generate API key button above the provider fields](/images/blog/announcing-bird-messaging-providers/one-click-bird-email-dark.avif)
{% /only_dark %}

{% only_light %}
![The Configure Bird step for the email provider after connecting Bird, with a verified domain selected and the Generate API key button above the provider fields](/images/blog/announcing-bird-messaging-providers/one-click-bird-email.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Appwrite creates a key named `Appwrite SMTP: <project name>` with only the `emails:write` scope. Bird keys aren't tied to a domain, so the domain you pick sets the sender: the sender email becomes `noreply@<domain>` unless you already typed an address on that domain, the sender name defaults to your project name, and the provider name defaults to `One-click Bird sender`.

To fill the form by hand instead, create a key under **Platform tools** > **API keys** in Bird with the `emails` scope at the `write` level and paste it into **API key**. The [Bird email docs](/docs/products/messaging/bird-email) cover both paths.

# Send SMS through Bird

The SMS provider needs a sender your workspace owns. On Bird's **Senders** page, buy a number or claim an alphanumeric sender ID, and enable the countries you send to under **SMS** > **Destinations**. Bird refuses messages to countries that aren't enabled, and the US doesn't accept alphanumeric sender IDs.

1. Go to **Messaging** > **Providers** > **Add provider** and choose **Bird** under **SMS**.
2. On Appwrite Cloud, click **Connect with Bird**. If you already connected Bird for email, the senders load straight away.
3. Pick a **Sender** and click **Generate API key**.
4. Review the fields and click **Create provider**.

{% only_dark %}
![The Configure Bird step for the SMS provider after connecting Bird, with a sender selected and the Generate API key button above the provider fields](/images/blog/announcing-bird-messaging-providers/one-click-bird-sms-dark.avif)
{% /only_dark %}

{% only_light %}
![The Configure Bird step for the SMS provider after connecting Bird, with a sender selected and the Generate API key button above the provider fields](/images/blog/announcing-bird-messaging-providers/one-click-bird-sms.avif)
{% /only_light %}

This key is named `Appwrite SMS: <project name>` and holds only `sms:write`, so the email key and the SMS key stay separate. You can revoke either in Bird without touching the other channel. The [Bird SMS docs](/docs/products/messaging/bird-sms) list the manual fields and the sender rules.

# Set up custom SMTP with Bird

Custom SMTP replaces Appwrite Cloud's default sender for authentication emails, such as verification, magic URL, and password recovery, and unlocks [custom email templates](/docs/products/auth/message-templates#customize). It's available on Pro and higher plans.

1. Open your project's **Settings** and go to the **SMTP** tab.
2. Under **One-click setup**, click **Connect with Bird** on the Bird tile.
3. Select a verified **Domain**, review the **Sender name** and **Sender email**, and click **Set up**.

{% only_dark %}
![The One-click setup card on the SMTP tab, with Resend, Bird, and Mailgun tiles above the Custom SMTP server form](/images/blog/announcing-bird-messaging-providers/smtp-one-click-setup-dark.avif)
{% /only_dark %}

{% only_light %}
![The One-click setup card on the SMTP tab, with Resend, Bird, and Mailgun tiles above the Custom SMTP server form](/images/blog/announcing-bird-messaging-providers/smtp-one-click-setup.avif)
{% /only_light %}

The setup dialog fills in your project name and `noreply@<domain>` as the sender, and its sidebar shows the relay settings before you confirm them.

{% only_dark %}
![The Set up SMTP with Bird dialog: a verified sending domain, the sender name and email, and a sidebar with the Bird relay host, port, username, and TLS](/images/blog/announcing-bird-messaging-providers/smtp-bird-wizard-dark.avif)
{% /only_dark %}

{% only_light %}
![The Set up SMTP with Bird dialog: a verified sending domain, the sender name and email, and a sidebar with the Bird relay host, port, username, and TLS](/images/blog/announcing-bird-messaging-providers/smtp-bird-wizard.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Appwrite creates a sending-only key and writes the relay settings for your workspace's region:

| Setting | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Server host | `<region>.smtp.bird.com`, for example `eu1.smtp.bird.com` |
| Server port | `587` |
| Username | `bird` |
| Secure protocol | TLS |
| Password | The `emails:write` key Appwrite creates |

Custom SMTP is enabled as soon as the setup finishes, and your existing reply-to address is kept. The full steps are in the [custom SMTP docs](/docs/products/auth/message-templates#smtp-one-click-setup).

# Create Bird providers from your backend

The Console is the quickest path, but you can also create the providers through the Messaging API. The `createBirdEmailProvider` and `createBirdSMSProvider` methods ship with the next release of each server SDK. Until then, call the REST endpoints with an API key that has the `providers.write` scope:

{% tabs %}
{% tabsitem #bird-email title="Bird email" %}
```http
POST /v1/messaging/providers/bird-email HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json
X-Appwrite-Project: <PROJECT_ID>
X-Appwrite-Key: <API_KEY>

{
  "providerId": "bird-email",
  "name": "Bird email",
  "apiKey": "<BIRD_API_KEY>",
  "fromName": "Acme",
  "fromEmail": "noreply@<YOUR_DOMAIN>",
  "enabled": true
}
```
{% /tabsitem %}
{% tabsitem #bird-sms title="Bird SMS" %}
```http
POST /v1/messaging/providers/bird-sms HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json
X-Appwrite-Project: <PROJECT_ID>
X-Appwrite-Key: <API_KEY>

{
  "providerId": "bird-sms",
  "name": "Bird SMS",
  "apiKey": "<BIRD_API_KEY>",
  "from": "+12065550100",
  "enabled": true
}
```
{% /tabsitem %}
{% /tabs %}

A provider is only enabled once it has everything it needs to send: an API key and a sender email for email, an API key and a sender for SMS. Each update endpoint only accepts its own channel, so `PATCH /v1/messaging/providers/bird-sms/:providerId` refuses an email provider with `provider_incorrect_type`.

Sending doesn't change. `createEmail` and `createSMS` work from every server SDK today, and messages go out through the provider each target is linked to, or through the project's first enabled provider of that type when the target has none. The [send email](/docs/products/messaging/send-email-messages) and [send SMS](/docs/products/messaging/send-sms-messages) guides walk through topics, targets, and scheduling.

# How Bird delivery works in Appwrite

A few details of the email provider are worth knowing before your first campaign:

- **One message per recipient.** Every recipient in **To** gets a message of their own, with CC and BCC copied on each. Several recipients go to Bird in batches of up to 100.
- **Failures stay per recipient.** Bird validates a batch as one unit and refuses all of it when one address is unacceptable. When that happens, Appwrite sends the batch again one message at a time, so only the refused recipient fails. This matters because Bird refuses placeholder domains such as `example.com`, which are common on test users.
- **Transactional by default.** Both providers send with Bird's `transactional` category. Bird's own email default is `marketing`, which adds an unsubscribe link and holds mail back from anyone who opted out or is suppressed for any reason, including password resets they asked for.
- **A 20 MB message limit.** Bird counts the body and attachments after base64 encoding, which leaves room for about 15 MB of files. Larger messages fail in Appwrite before reaching Bird.

For tests without a real inbox, [Bird's mail sandbox](https://bird.com/docs/guides/email/testing-sandbox) accepts addresses such as `delivered@messagebird.dev` through the normal API.

# How the Bird connection works

**Connect with Bird** runs an OAuth2 flow from the Console to Bird. A Bird authorization grants access to one workspace rather than to a person, so Appwrite links it to the Console account you're signed in to and never uses it to sign anyone in.

The authorization is broader than any key it creates, because listing domains and senders and creating keys need it. That access stays on your Console account. Your project only ever stores the single-scope key Appwrite creates, which you can find in Bird by its `Appwrite SMTP` or `Appwrite SMS` name.

Keys don't linger either. If you leave the provider wizard without creating the provider, Appwrite revokes the key it generated, and clicking **Generate API key** again revokes the previous one. Disconnecting Bird from the SMTP tab removes the Bird identity from your Console account without changing settings already saved on your projects.

# When to reach for Bird

- **Your team already runs email or SMS on Bird.** Keep your verified domains, senders, and delivery reporting in one place, and let Appwrite send through them.
- **You want email and SMS from one vendor.** A single Bird workspace covers both Messaging providers and custom SMTP, with a separate key per channel.
- **Your messaging needs to stay in one region.** Each Bird workspace lives in one region, `eu1` or `us1` today, and Appwrite only sends to the region named in your key.

If you already send through another provider, there's no reason to switch. Appwrite Messaging supports [several email and SMS providers](/docs/products/messaging/providers), and you can run more than one in the same project.

# Get started with Bird in Appwrite Messaging

The Bird email and SMS providers and one-click Bird setup are live on [Appwrite Cloud](/sign-in) today. The providers ship in the next self-hosted Appwrite release. Open **Messaging** > **Providers**, choose **Bird**, and click **Connect with Bird**.

